D={
"Ramanuja":("Ramanuja (traditionally 1017–1137) was a Tamil Vaishnava philosopher who taught Vishishtadvaita ('qualified non-dualism'): the soul and world are real, distinct parts of Brahman (Vishnu), and liberation comes through devotion (bhakti) and surrender. He reorganised worship at Srirangam and, according to tradition, shared the sacred mantra publicly from a temple tower so all castes could be saved. He is said to have fled Chola persecution to the Hoysala court, where he converted King Vishnuvardhana.","Hinduism; Bhakti movement; Chola Empire; Hoysala Empire; Adi Shankaracharya"),
"Basava":("Basava (Basavanna, 1105–1167), a minister of the Kalachuri king Bijjala at Kalyana in Karnataka, founded the Lingayat (Veerashaiva) movement. He rejected caste, temple ritual and gender discrimination, teaching devotion to Shiva symbolised by a personal linga, and that 'work is worship' (kayaka). His Anubhava Mantapa gathered saints of all castes, including women like Akka Mahadevi, who composed vachanas, free-verse poems in Kannada. A marriage between a Brahmin girl and a Dalit boy sparked violence that ended the community at Kalyana.","Hinduism; Bhakti movement; Western Chalukyas (Kalyani); Kannada"),
"Rani Rudramadevi":("Rudramadevi (ruled about 1262–1289) was one of the few women to rule an Indian kingdom in her own right. Her father, the Kakatiya king Ganapati, named her heir and she was raised as a prince. She defeated rebellious nobles and invasions by the Yadavas of Devagiri and the Eastern Gangas, completed Warangal fort, and built tanks for irrigation. Marco Polo praised her rule. Her grandson Prataparudra succeeded her.","Kakatiya dynasty; Hinduism; Marco Polo in China"),
"Amir Khusrau":("Amir Khusrau (1253–1325), a poet, musician and Sufi disciple of Nizamuddin Auliya in Delhi, served seven Delhi sultans. He wrote in Persian and Hindavi, composing ghazals, riddles and histories of Alauddin Khalji's campaigns. He is credited with developing qawwali and the tarana, and legends credit him with inventing the sitar and tabla, though historians doubt this. He called himself the 'Parrot of India'.","Delhi Sultanate; Khalji dynasty; Tughlaq dynasty; Sufism; Islam; Hindi–Urdu (Hindustani)"),
"Ibn Khaldun":("Ibn Khaldun (1332–1406), born in Tunis, was a scholar, judge and diplomat in North Africa, Spain and Egypt. His Muqaddimah (1377) analysed why dynasties rise and fall: tribal groups with strong solidarity (asabiyyah) conquer settled societies, then grow soft and lose power within about four generations. He is seen as a founder of sociology and economics. In 1401 he met Timur outside Damascus.","Islam; Timurid Empire; Mamluk Sultanate (Egypt); Black Death"),
"Zheng He":("Zheng He (1371–1433), born to a Muslim family in Yunnan, was captured as a boy by Ming armies, castrated and became a eunuch servant of Prince Zhu Di (later the Yongle Emperor). He commanded seven 'treasure fleet' voyages (1405–1433) with up to 300 ships and 27,000 men, visiting Java, Sri Lanka, Calicut, Hormuz, Mecca and East Africa, and bringing back envoys and a giraffe. After his death, the Ming abandoned the voyages.","Ming dynasty; Zheng He's voyages; Islam; Malacca Sultanate"),
"Joan of Arc":("Joan of Arc (about 1412–1431), a peasant girl from Domrémy, said saints' voices told her to help Charles VII drive the English out of France during the Hundred Years' War. At 17 she led troops to lift the siege of Orléans (1429) and saw Charles crowned at Reims. Captured by Burgundians and handed to the English, she was tried for heresy and burned at Rouen at 19. She was declared innocent in 1456 and made a saint in 1920.","Hundred Years' War; Christianity; Normans and Plantagenets"),
"Rana Pratap":("Maharana Pratap Singh (1540–1597) of Mewar refused to accept Mughal overlordship, unlike many Rajput rulers. At Haldighati in 1576 he fought Akbar's army led by Man Singh; his horse Chetak carried him to safety before dying. Living in the Aravalli hills with Bhil allies and supported by the merchant Bhamashah's wealth, he waged guerrilla war and regained much of Mewar except Chittor. He remains a symbol of Rajput pride.","Battle of Haldighati; Rajput kingdoms of Mewar (Sisodia); Akbar; Mughal Empire; Bhil; Hinduism"),
"Chand Bibi":("Chand Bibi (1550–1599), a princess of Ahmadnagar married into Bijapur's royal family, served as regent of Bijapur and later of Ahmadnagar. In 1595 she defended Ahmadnagar fort against Akbar's son Murad, rallying troops and repairing a breached wall overnight, forcing a treaty. She was killed in 1599, reportedly by her own soldiers, before the Mughals took the fort.","Bahmani Sultanate; Mughal Empire; Akbar; Islam"),
"Rani Abbakka Chowta":("Abbakka Chowta, the 16th-century Tuluva queen of Ullal near Mangalore, resisted Portuguese attempts to control the coast's trade and demand tribute. A Jain who ruled a mixed Hindu–Muslim–Jain population, she allied with the Zamorin of Calicut and Bijapur. She fought the Portuguese for decades, reportedly using fire arrows at night, before being captured. She is celebrated as one of India's first freedom fighters.","Portuguese Empire; Portuguese take Goa; Jainism; Vijayanagara Empire"),
"Lachit Borphukan":("Lachit Borphukan (1622–1672) was commander of the Ahom army. In 1671, though seriously ill, he led the Ahoms to defeat a much larger Mughal fleet under Raja Ram Singh at the Battle of Saraighat on the Brahmaputra near Guwahati, using river warfare and fortifications. Legends say he beheaded his own uncle for failing to complete a defensive wall overnight. Lachit Divas is celebrated on 24 November.","Ahom kingdom; Ahom dynasty; Mughal Empire; Hinduism"),
"Ahilyabai Holkar":("Ahilyabai Holkar (1725–1795) was widowed at 29 and lost her father-in-law Malhar Rao and son, then took over as ruler of the Holkar state of Malwa from Maheshwar. She ruled for 28 years with justice and prosperity, defended her lands and built or restored hundreds of temples, ghats and rest houses across India, including the Kashi Vishwanath temple in Varanasi (1780) and Somnath. Indore's airport and university are named after her.","Maratha Confederacy; Maratha Empire (Bhonsle and Peshwas); Hinduism"),
"Toussaint Louverture":("François-Dominique Toussaint Louverture (about 1743–1803), born enslaved in Saint-Domingue, gained his freedom and became the leader of the 1791 slave revolt. A skilled general, he defeated Spanish and British forces, abolished slavery and governed the colony. Napoleon sent an army to restore slavery; Toussaint was captured by trickery and died in a French prison in 1803. Haiti won independence months later.","Haitian Revolution; French Revolution; Napoleon Bonaparte; Atlantic slave trade"),
"Simón Bolívar":("Simón Bolívar (1783–1830), a wealthy Venezuelan creole, swore to free South America from Spanish rule. After defeats and exile, he crossed the Andes in 1819 to win at Boyacá, then won at Carabobo (1821) and with Sucre at Ayacucho (1824). He liberated Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia (named after him) and led Gran Colombia, but his dream of a united South America collapsed amid regional rivalry before his death.","Latin American independence; Spanish Empire; Napoleonic Wars"),
"Velu Nachiyar":("Velu Nachiyar (1730–1796), princess of Ramanathapuram, was trained in martial arts and languages. After the East India Company and the Nawab of Arcot killed her husband, the king of Sivaganga, in 1772, she escaped, allied with Hyder Ali, and in 1780 recaptured Sivaganga. Legend says her commander Kuyili set herself on fire to destroy a British ammunition store. She is called Veeramangai ('brave woman').","East India Company rule; Kingdom of Mysore; Tipu Sultan"),
"Hippocrates":("Hippocrates of Kos (about 460–370 BCE) is called the father of Western medicine. He and his school argued diseases have natural causes rather than divine punishment, and stressed careful observation, diet and the 'four humours'. The Hippocratic Corpus contains about 60 works, and the Hippocratic Oath, with principles like 'do no harm', still inspires medical ethics.","Classical Greece; Plague of Athens; Charaka; Sushruta"),
"Euclid":("Euclid of Alexandria (about 300 BCE), working in the Ptolemaic capital's Museum, wrote the Elements, 13 books building geometry and number theory from definitions and five postulates. It proved that there are infinitely many primes and constructed the five Platonic solids. It was the main geometry textbook for over 2,000 years, translated into Arabic and Latin.","Ptolemaic dynasty; Classical Greece; Archimedes; Founding of Baghdad and the House of Wisdom"),
"Xuanzang":("Xuanzang (602–664), a Chinese Buddhist monk, left Chang'an in 629 without permission to seek authentic texts. He crossed the Gobi and Central Asia, studied for years at Nalanda, debated at the court of Harsha, and returned in 645 with 657 texts, which he spent his life translating. His Great Tang Records on the Western Regions guided archaeologists to Indian sites, and he inspired the novel Journey to the West.","Tang dynasty; Xuanzang's journey to India; Nalanda founded; Harsha; Buddhism; Pushyabhuti (Vardhana) dynasty"),
}
